cartridge "CRAB FIGHT/BUCK CHILDRESS/1986"

' CRAB FIGHT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Crabfight" by Buck Childress, Ahoy!
' February 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000-$CB07, POKEd in
' by a four-line BASIC loader; author from LaunchBox, the listing names
' none). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in
' Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/crab.dis), the game is built for the ColecoVision: a
' sandy beach between rocks with a coral pool and a kelp pool at the ends,
' two smooth crabs that scuttle and snap their claws, a clam, wiggling
' octopuses, sound effects.
'
' The game: a red crab (top) and a purple crab (bottom) race for the clam
' in the middle. The crab that touches it holds it, and must throw it
' within three seconds (the clock beside the field runs down) or the other
' crab gets 10 points and the clam goes back to the middle. A thrown clam
' bounces off the rocks at the sides; when it reaches the far end wall the
' thrower scores 5, and when it hits one of the two octopuses in the other
' crab's pool the thrower scores 20 (that octopus hides until the clam is
' back in the middle of the beach, and the clam turns back). Each crab
' stays in its half. The first to 200 points wins.
'
' From the original (orig/): the loop's step that moves the clam 1 pixel
' up or down and 2 sideways in one of six directions, the crabs 2 pixels
' sideways and 1 up or down per step; the throw codes (button + forward,
' left or right; no movement while the button is held); the bounces and
' their scoring (5 at an end wall, 20 an octopus with the clam sent back,
' the octopus back when the clam is in the middle band); possession by
' touch; the clock of three
' digits; 10 to the other crab when it runs out, then a new serve with
' five beeps; first to 200. The computer (one player) is the original's
' red crab: it follows the clam sideways, backs off when the clam is in
' its half, comes forward to the middle (or all the way at a serve), goes
' to the middle when the other crab holds the clam, and when it holds the
' clam it backs off, sweeps from side to side and throws when the jiffy
' clock's low byte is under 96, straight or diagonally at random; its
' level makes it skip one step in 3, 4 or 5 (EASY, MEDIUM, HARD).
'
' Liberties: the C64's loop ran 5.5, 3.5 or 2.5 steps a frame (levels 1-3,
' worked out from its delay loops); here SLOW, MEDIUM and FAST run 1.5,
' 2 and 2.5 (FAST is the C64's slowest), checked with a human-paced bot;
' the field is 160 x 176 pixels (the C64's 168 x 184), the crabs 24 x 16
' and the clam 12 x 12, touches are box tests (the C64 used its sprite
' collision register); a clam touching both crabs goes to each in turn
' (the C64 gave it to neither, which could stall a serve for ever); the clock counts whole seconds (the C64 counted 256
' steps a digit); the computer keeps inside the rocks and its throws start
' on an even pixel; the crab opens its claws while the button is held (the
' C64 turned it blue); one player uses either controller and plays the
' purple crab, as on the C64's port 2; the C64's pause key (RUN/STOP, then
' N new or C continue) is left out; the winner's message, the number of
' throws, then the title (the C64 asked N or C).
'
' Controls: one player: controller 1 or 2, the purple crab (bottom). Two
' players: controller 1 the purple crab, controller 2 the red crab (top).
' Stick: move. Hold a button and push the stick forward, left or right to
' throw the clam. On the title: up and down choose a line, left and right
' (or a button) change it, a button on START plays.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

